# Metrics

This app exposes framework-owned metrics through `github.com/goforj/metrics`.

Metrics are emitted by the generated framework wiring, not by ad-hoc application code. The goal is to make the app observable by default while keeping labels bounded and safe for production.

## Endpoints

Standalone host mode (`app run`) prefers one shared metrics endpoint:

- Host metrics: `http://localhost:9100/metrics`

Direct source commands keep their dedicated listeners:

{{- if or .Components.WebAPI .Components.WebUI }}
- HTTP metrics: `http://localhost:9100/metrics`
{{- end }}
{{- if .Components.Jobs }}
- Jobs metrics: `http://localhost:9101/metrics`
{{- end }}
{{- if .Components.Scheduler }}
- Scheduler metrics: `http://localhost:9102/metrics`
{{- end }}
{{- if .Components.WebAPI }}
- App HTTP route: `GET /metrics`
  - Prometheus text exposition
  - served without auth by default
{{- end }}

In standalone host mode, `app run` uses one shared metrics endpoint and hosts multiple logical sources in one process.

Direct source commands keep dedicated metrics listeners so HTTP, jobs, and scheduler can still be scraped independently in distributed or process-split layouts.

Framework-owned metric families should be queried by `source` when that label is present. The generated observability stack also adds scrape-time `process` labels so host mode and direct source commands remain easy to distinguish operationally:

- standalone host mode: `process=app`
- direct source mode: `process=http`, `process=jobs`, `process=scheduler`

Dedicated metrics listeners are intentionally separate from normal application traffic. This keeps scrape traffic out of HTTP request metrics and preserves clean source-specific scrape topology when you run leaf commands directly.

## Runtime Toggles

Each framework-owned instrumentation surface can be enabled or disabled independently at startup:

- `METRICS_HTTP_ENABLED`
- `METRICS_CACHE_ENABLED`
- `METRICS_STORAGE_ENABLED`
- `METRICS_EVENTS_ENABLED`
{{- if .Components.Mail }}
- `METRICS_MAIL_ENABLED`
{{- end }}
{{- if .Components.Jobs }}
- `METRICS_QUEUE_ENABLED`
{{- end }}
{{- if .Components.HasDatabase }}
- `METRICS_DATABASE_ENABLED`
{{- end }}
{{- if .Components.Auth }}
- `METRICS_AUTH_ENABLED`
{{- end }}
{{- if .Components.Scheduler }}
- `METRICS_SCHEDULER_ENABLED`
{{- end }}

All of these default to `true`. Observer-backed primitives such as cache, storage, events, mail, and queue avoid attaching metrics observers when their toggle is disabled.

## Metric Families

Prometheus names use underscores and suffixes. For example, the GoForj metric `http.requests.by_route` is exported as `http_requests_by_route_total`.

| Area | Metric families | Key labels |
| --- | --- | --- |
| HTTP | `http_requests_total`, `http_requests_by_route_total`, `http_request_duration_seconds`, `http_request_duration_by_route_seconds`, `http_requests_inflight`, `http_responses_client_errors_total`, `http_responses_server_errors_total` | `source`, `method`, `route`, `status` |
| Auth | `auth_flows_total`, `auth_flow_duration_seconds` | `flow`, `status`, `reason`, `provider` |
| Cache | `cache_operations_total`, `cache_operation_duration_seconds` | `source`, `cache`, `driver`, `operation`, `category`, `status`, `hit` |
| Storage | `storage_operations_total`, `storage_operation_duration_seconds` | `source`, `disk`, `driver`, `operation`, `category`, `status` |
| Events | `events_publishes_total`, `events_publish_duration_seconds`, `events_subscriptions_total`, `events_subscribers_active`, `events_deliveries_total`, `events_deliveries_inflight`, `events_delivery_duration_seconds` | `source`, `bus`, `driver`, `topic`, `handler`, `status`, `reason` |
| Mail | `mail_sends_total`, `mail_send_duration_seconds` | `source`, `mailer`, `driver`, `status`, `reason` |
| Queue | `queue_jobs_enqueued_total`, `queue_jobs_scheduled_total`, `queue_jobs_processed_total`, `queue_jobs_failed_total`, `queue_jobs_retried_total`, `queue_jobs_archived_total`, `queue_jobs_by_job_total`, `queue_jobs_inflight`, `queue_jobs_inflight_by_job`, `queue_events_by_queue_total`, `queue_job_duration_by_queue_seconds`, `queue_job_duration_by_job_seconds` | `source`, `queue`, `job_name`, `driver`, `status`, `kind` |
| Scheduler | `scheduler_started_total`, `scheduler_runs_total`, `scheduler_failures_total`, `scheduler_skips_total`, `scheduler_runs_by_job_total`, `scheduler_events_by_job_total`, `scheduler_jobs_inflight`, `scheduler_job_duration_by_job_seconds` | `source`, `job_name`, `target_kind`, `status`, `reason` |
| Database | `database_operations_total`, `database_operation_duration_seconds`, `database_queries_by_fingerprint_total`, `database_query_fingerprint_info`, `database_slow_queries_total`, `database_slow_query_duration_seconds`, `database_pool_open`, `database_pool_in_use`, `database_pool_idle`, `database_pool_wait_count_total`, `database_pool_wait_duration_total`, `database_pool_closed_max_idle_total`, `database_pool_closed_max_idle_time_total`, `database_pool_closed_max_lifetime_total` | `connection`, `driver`, `operation`, `target`, `status`, `fingerprint`, `query_shape` |

The local observability stack adds a `process` label at scrape time. Typical values are:

- `app` for standalone host mode
- `http`, `jobs`, and `scheduler` for direct source mode

Some framework-owned metric families also emit a `source` label directly when the logical source is known at emission time. Today that includes HTTP, queue, scheduler, cache, storage, events, and mail metrics. Prefer `source` for logical attribution and `process` for scrape-target topology.

When a framework observer path cannot preserve source context truthfully, source-aware metric families fall back to `source="app"` rather than surfacing noisy placeholder labels in dashboards.

Component-specific metric families are emitted when the corresponding component is enabled. Core primitive metrics such as cache, storage, and events are available for generated sources that wire those primitives.

## Labels

Labels are intentionally low-cardinality:

- `route` uses the registered route pattern, such as `/api/users/:id`, not the raw URL.
- `cache`, `disk`, `mailer`, `queue`, `connection`, and `bus` use named accessors or configured primitive names.
- `target` for database metrics is the normalized table or query target when available.
- `job_name` is a stable queue or scheduler job type/name.
- `handler` is the stable event handler identity.
- `hit` uses `hit`, `miss`, or `na` instead of raw booleans.
- `status`, `reason`, `operation`, and `category` are normalized framework-owned values.

Avoid adding labels for user IDs, emails, session IDs, cache keys, filenames, request IDs, raw SQL, raw URLs, or arbitrary error strings. Those values create high-cardinality metrics that are expensive to store and hard to query.

## Custom App Metrics

The generated framework metrics cover platform behavior such as HTTP, auth, queue, scheduler, cache, storage, and database activity. Your application can add its own business metrics on top through the same `github.com/goforj/metrics` registry.

The flow is:

1. define custom metrics in an application-owned package
2. inject the generated app metrics manager into that package
3. record from the service, handler, or job where the business event actually happens
4. query the new series from the existing `/metrics` endpoint and Grafana stack

The generated app already exposes the shared metrics manager:

```go
app.Metrics()
```

That means your custom metrics should register against the existing registry instead of creating a second one.

### Example: Counter For Orders Created

Use a counter when you want to record how many times something happened.

Real-world fit:

- orders created
- invoices paid
- password reset emails sent

Application-owned metric wrapper:

```go
package billing

import (
	gmetrics "github.com/goforj/metrics"
	"example.com/app/internal/metrics"
)

type Metrics struct {
	ordersCreated *gmetrics.CounterVec
}

func NewMetrics(manager *metrics.Manager) *Metrics {
	registry := manager.Registry()
	return &Metrics{
		ordersCreated: registry.MustCounterVec(gmetrics.Descriptor{
			Name: "billing.orders.created",
			Help: "Total orders created by checkout outcome.",
			Kind: gmetrics.KindCounter,
		}, []string{"channel", "status"}),
	}
}

func (m *Metrics) RecordOrderCreated(channel, status string) {
	m.ordersCreated.WithLabelValues(channel, status).Inc()
}
```

Service usage:

```go
package billing

type Service struct {
	repo    *OrderRepo
	metrics *Metrics
}

func NewService(repo *OrderRepo, metrics *Metrics) *Service {
	return &Service{repo: repo, metrics: metrics}
}

func (s *Service) CreateOrder(in CreateOrderInput) error {
	if err := s.repo.Save(in); err != nil {
		s.metrics.RecordOrderCreated("web", "failed")
		return err
	}
	s.metrics.RecordOrderCreated("web", "succeeded")
	return nil
}
```

What this gives you:

- `billing_orders_created_total{channel="web",status="succeeded"}`

### Example: Gauge For In-Flight Sync Jobs

Use a gauge when a value goes up and down over time.

Real-world fit:

- active export jobs
- current websocket sessions
- open checkout sessions

Application-owned metric wrapper:

```go
package syncer

import (
	gmetrics "github.com/goforj/metrics"
	"example.com/app/internal/metrics"
)

type Metrics struct {
	inflight *gmetrics.GaugeVec
}

func NewMetrics(manager *metrics.Manager) *Metrics {
	registry := manager.Registry()
	return &Metrics{
		inflight: registry.MustGaugeVec(gmetrics.Descriptor{
			Name: "sync.jobs.inflight",
			Help: "Current sync jobs in progress by provider.",
			Kind: gmetrics.KindGauge,
		}, []string{"provider"}),
	}
}

func (m *Metrics) JobStarted(provider string) {
	m.inflight.WithLabelValues(provider).Add(1)
}

func (m *Metrics) JobFinished(provider string) {
	m.inflight.WithLabelValues(provider).Add(-1)
}
```

Job usage:

```go
func (s *Service) RunHubSpotSync() error {
	s.metrics.JobStarted("hubspot")
	defer s.metrics.JobFinished("hubspot")

	return s.syncHubSpotAccounts()
}
```

What this gives you:

- `sync_jobs_inflight{provider="hubspot"}`

### Example: Histogram For Checkout Latency

Use a histogram when you care about latency or value distributions.

Real-world fit:

- checkout duration
- report generation time
- third-party API round-trip time

Application-owned metric wrapper:

```go
package checkout

import (
	"time"

	gmetrics "github.com/goforj/metrics"
	"example.com/app/internal/metrics"
)

type Metrics struct {
	duration *gmetrics.HistogramVec
}

func NewMetrics(manager *metrics.Manager) *Metrics {
	registry := manager.Registry()
	return &Metrics{
		duration: registry.MustHistogramVec(gmetrics.Descriptor{
			Name: "checkout.duration",
			Help: "Checkout completion latency by payment provider and outcome.",
			Kind: gmetrics.KindHistogram,
			Unit: gmetrics.UnitSeconds,
		}, []string{"provider", "status"}, gmetrics.DurationBounds(gmetrics.DefaultDurationBounds())),
	}
}

func (m *Metrics) RecordDuration(provider, status string, dur time.Duration) {
	m.duration.WithLabelValues(provider, status).Observe(dur.Seconds())
}
```

Service usage:

```go
func (s *Service) CompleteCheckout(in CompleteCheckoutInput) error {
	startedAt := time.Now()
	err := s.gateway.Charge(in)
	if err != nil {
		s.metrics.RecordDuration("stripe", "failed", time.Since(startedAt))
		return err
	}
	s.metrics.RecordDuration("stripe", "succeeded", time.Since(startedAt))
	return nil
}
```

What this gives you:

- `checkout_duration_seconds_bucket{provider="stripe",status="succeeded",le="..."}`
- `checkout_duration_seconds_count{provider="stripe",status="succeeded"}`
- `checkout_duration_seconds_sum{provider="stripe",status="succeeded"}`

### Where To Wire It

Keep the metrics definitions close to the domain they describe. A practical shape is:

- `internal/billing/metrics.go`
- `internal/checkout/metrics.go`
- `internal/syncer/metrics.go`

Then inject the generated metrics manager into your domain-owned metrics wrapper and service constructor.

Example:

```go
package billing

import "example.com/app/internal/metrics"

func NewMetrics(manager *metrics.Manager) *Metrics {
	// register custom billing metrics here
}

func NewService(repo *OrderRepo, metrics *Metrics) *Service {
	return &Service{repo: repo, metrics: metrics}
}
```

The important rule is:

- register metrics once during application startup
- record them from the domain logic that owns the event

Do not create new counters or histograms inside request handlers or inside hot code paths per call.

### Label Guidance

Good labels:

- `status`
- `provider`
- `channel`
- `plan`
- `region`

Bad labels:

- `user_id`
- `email`
- `order_id`
- `request_id`
- `error_message`
- `filename`

Keep labels bounded and predictable. If a label can explode into thousands or millions of distinct values, it should probably not be a metric label.

## Common Queries

These examples work in Grafana Explore or VictoriaMetrics.

### HTTP

Request rate by route:

```promql
sum by (route, method) (
  rate(http_requests_by_route_total[$__rate_interval])
)
```

Client/server error hotspots:

```promql
topk(10,
  sum by (route, status) (
    rate(http_requests_by_route_total{status=~"4..|5.."}[$__rate_interval])
  )
)
```

Route latency P95:

```promql
1000 * histogram_quantile(0.95,
  sum by (le, route) (
    rate(http_request_duration_by_route_seconds_bucket[$__rate_interval])
  )
)
```

### Cache

Hit rate by named cache:

```promql
100 *
sum by (cache) (rate(cache_operations_total{category="read",hit="hit"}[$__rate_interval]))
/
clamp_min(sum by (cache) (rate(cache_operations_total{category="read",hit=~"hit|miss"}[$__rate_interval])), 0.001)
```

Miss hotspots:

```promql
topk(10,
  sum by (process, cache, operation) (
    rate(cache_operations_total{hit="miss"}[$__rate_interval])
  )
)
```

### Storage

Read/list pressure by disk:

```promql
sum by (process, disk, operation) (
  rate(storage_operations_total{category=~"read|list",status="ok"}[$__rate_interval])
)
```

Write/delete/transfer pressure by disk:

```promql
sum by (process, disk, operation) (
  rate(storage_operations_total{category=~"write|delete|transfer|access",status="ok"}[$__rate_interval])
)
```

Storage error hotspots:

```promql
topk(10,
  sum by (disk, operation, status) (
    rate(storage_operations_total{status!="ok"}[$__rate_interval])
  )
)
```

### Database

Slowest tables by P95:

```promql
1000 * histogram_quantile(0.95,
  sum by (le, connection, target, operation) (
    rate(database_operation_duration_seconds_bucket[$__rate_interval])
  )
)
```

Pool utilization by connection:

```promql
100 *
sum by (connection) (database_pool_in_use)
/
clamp_min(sum by (connection) (database_pool_open), 1)
```

Top query fingerprints by volume:

```promql
topk(10,
  sum by (connection, target, fingerprint) (
    increase(database_queries_by_fingerprint_total[$__range])
  )
)
```

Slow query fingerprints by volume:

```promql
topk(10,
  sum by (connection, target, fingerprint) (
    increase(database_slow_queries_total[$__range])
  )
)
```

The slow query threshold is controlled by `DB_SLOW_QUERY_THRESHOLD`. Fingerprints are derived from normalized SQL shape, not raw SQL text, so similar statements group together without exposing literals as metric labels.
For Grafana lookup, `database_query_fingerprint_info` exposes a bounded `query_shape` label. Long but safe shapes are truncated, and unsafe shapes fall back to `other`.

### Queue

Completed jobs by queue and job type:

```promql
sum by (queue, job_name) (
  increase(queue_jobs_by_job_total{status="succeeded"}[$__range])
)
```

Retry/failure pressure:

```promql
topk(10,
  sum by (queue, job_name, status) (
    rate(queue_jobs_by_job_total{status=~"failed|retried|archived"}[$__rate_interval])
  )
)
```

### Scheduler

Run outcomes by scheduled job:

```promql
sum by (job_name, status) (
  increase(scheduler_runs_by_job_total[$__range])
)
```

Skipped schedule reasons:

```promql
sum by (job_name, reason) (
  increase(scheduler_events_by_job_total{status="skipped"}[$__range])
)
```

### Events

Publish and delivery throughput by topic:

```promql
sum by (process, topic) (
  rate(events_publishes_total{status="ok"}[$__rate_interval])
)
```

```promql
sum by (process, topic) (
  rate(events_deliveries_total{status="ok"}[$__rate_interval])
)
```

Delivery lag symptom, where publishes are happening but deliveries are not keeping up:

```promql
sum by (topic) (rate(events_publishes_total{status="ok"}[$__rate_interval]))
-
sum by (topic) (rate(events_deliveries_total{status="ok"}[$__rate_interval]))
```

Handler latency P95:

```promql
1000 * histogram_quantile(0.95,
  sum by (le, topic, handler) (
    rate(events_delivery_duration_seconds_bucket[$__rate_interval])
  )
)
```

### Mail

Failure rate by mailer:

```promql
100 *
sum by (mailer) (rate(mail_sends_total{status=~"error|timeout"}[$__rate_interval]))
/
clamp_min(sum by (mailer) (rate(mail_sends_total[$__rate_interval])), 0.001)
```

## Source Notes

- Metrics are registered during app startup through DI.
- Export uses snapshot-based Prometheus encoding.
- `/metrics` and Lighthouse source routes are excluded from application HTTP request metrics.
- Ultra-fast in-process primitives may report microsecond or sub-millisecond latencies; this is expected for memory-backed drivers.
- Additional app-defined metrics can be registered through the shared metrics manager.
